00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:25.130
&gt;&gt; Thanks, Neil. Hello, everyone.

00:00:25.131 --> 00:00:26.765
Today, I'm going to talk about

00:00:26.766 --> 00:00:29.135
Automated Reasoning
of Database Queries.

00:00:29.136 --> 00:00:31.430
This is joint work with Konstantin,

00:00:31.431 --> 00:00:35.400
Chenglong, Daniel, Brendan,
Jared, Alvin, and Dan.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:38.970
First to motivate us to
talk a little bit well.

00:00:38.971 --> 00:00:48.210
So, this is a figure I
scrapped from Stack Overflow.

00:00:48.211 --> 00:00:53.235
Well, it shows that SQL is the
second popular program languages.

00:00:53.236 --> 00:00:56.079
The first one is JavaScript.

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:02.490
SQL is great because it actually has

00:01:02.491 --> 00:01:06.450
a restricted abstraction that
enables powerful optimizations.

00:01:06.451 --> 00:01:08.580
Well, during the 30 years of

00:01:08.581 --> 00:01:12.875
database research resulting
many powerful optimizations,

00:01:12.876 --> 00:01:16.790
which is based on semantic
equivalent of SQL rewrite.

00:01:16.791 --> 00:01:19.160
However, there's lacking tools that

00:01:19.161 --> 00:01:21.935
can actually reason
about SQL equivalences.

00:01:21.936 --> 00:01:26.395
Well, I usually use a
self-driving car analogy.

00:01:26.396 --> 00:01:29.885
It's embarrassing that we have
self-driving car right now,

00:01:29.886 --> 00:01:32.900
we still don't have
an automated solver for SQL.

00:01:32.901 --> 00:01:35.870
What exactly an automated
solver for SQL is?

00:01:35.871 --> 00:01:39.490
Is that for any possible input,

00:01:39.491 --> 00:01:42.000
I would want to check that whether

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:45.855
this Q one is equivalent to Q two.

00:01:45.856 --> 00:01:52.500
Well, this has a series of
interesting applications.

00:01:52.501 --> 00:01:55.085
For example, it can be used to verify

00:01:55.086 --> 00:01:57.590
the correctness of a query rewrite.

00:01:57.591 --> 00:02:01.205
So, you know that in
current landscape,

00:02:01.206 --> 00:02:03.590
the database system
is used to develop by

00:02:03.591 --> 00:02:06.345
big companies like Microsoft, IBM.

00:02:06.346 --> 00:02:08.310
However, there is more right now,

00:02:08.311 --> 00:02:12.620
like every open source community,

00:02:12.621 --> 00:02:13.790
like every start-up company are

00:02:13.791 --> 00:02:15.650
developing their own database system.

00:02:15.651 --> 00:02:19.430
How to make sure those
database system are as

00:02:19.431 --> 00:02:23.945
reliable as the ones developed
with big company is a big issue.

00:02:23.946 --> 00:02:26.510
Second, it could be used for

00:02:26.511 --> 00:02:31.025
a semantic caching layer
for data systems.

00:02:31.026 --> 00:02:34.045
Third, it could be used for

00:02:34.046 --> 00:02:37.645
automatic grading on the
data management assignment.

00:02:37.646 --> 00:02:40.450
We need to scale the grading because

00:02:40.451 --> 00:02:44.240
the limiting factor
is usually the human.

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:48.720
This is actually
a very challenging problem.

00:02:48.721 --> 00:02:53.005
So, we have this theoretical
result dated back on 50 years ago.

00:02:53.006 --> 00:02:55.300
Basically, he said that
checking the equivalence of

00:02:55.301 --> 00:02:59.464
first others sentence over
finite model is undecidable.

00:02:59.465 --> 00:03:03.130
While SQL is a super set

00:03:03.131 --> 00:03:06.310
of first order sentences because

00:03:06.311 --> 00:03:10.000
you can actually encode
first order sentencing in SQL.

00:03:10.001 --> 00:03:12.670
This is directly
applies that to check

00:03:12.671 --> 00:03:15.750
into SQL queries are undecidable.

00:03:15.751 --> 00:03:21.060
Also, SQL has rich language features.

00:03:21.061 --> 00:03:24.420
You might think SQL
just select from where,

00:03:24.421 --> 00:03:26.580
actually there is way more.

00:03:26.581 --> 00:03:29.610
For example, like in SQL,

00:03:29.611 --> 00:03:33.690
you have aggregation and
grouping, like index,

00:03:33.691 --> 00:03:34.995
you can specify index,

00:03:34.996 --> 00:03:36.710
you can specify AT query constraints

00:03:36.711 --> 00:03:38.780
like a primary key foreign keys,

00:03:38.781 --> 00:03:41.790
and you can write to
correlate the subqueries.

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:44.895
Well, to solve this problem,

00:03:44.896 --> 00:03:47.540
we basically have two observations.

00:03:47.541 --> 00:03:54.005
First, from result, undecidability
doesn't mean there is no proof.

00:03:54.006 --> 00:03:59.910
Actually, there is a development in

00:03:59.911 --> 00:04:02.670
formal method called
Interactive Theorem Prover that

00:04:02.671 --> 00:04:06.535
can actually validate
the mechanized proof.

00:04:06.536 --> 00:04:07.930
The problem here is that,

00:04:07.931 --> 00:04:10.390
how can we generate

00:04:10.391 --> 00:04:15.995
these mechanized proof automatically
for a pair of SQL queries?

00:04:15.996 --> 00:04:20.000
The second observation
is that we find that in

00:04:20.001 --> 00:04:21.890
practice is a model of

00:04:21.891 --> 00:04:25.625
an inequivalence SQL query
is usually not very large.

00:04:25.626 --> 00:04:29.410
This is actually known
to the formal methods,

00:04:29.411 --> 00:04:32.280
researchers as
a small world phenomenon,

00:04:32.281 --> 00:04:34.684
and this is partially explains why

00:04:34.685 --> 00:04:38.620
constraints nowadays
are extremely fast.

00:04:38.621 --> 00:04:42.375
So why not just reduce a problem

00:04:42.376 --> 00:04:44.125
and compel SQL to constraint

00:04:44.126 --> 00:04:46.855
in a constraint solver
for model checking?

00:04:46.856 --> 00:04:50.680
So based on these two observations,

00:04:50.681 --> 00:04:54.810
we construct almost
automated solver for

00:04:54.811 --> 00:04:57.080
SQL by combining interactive theorem

00:04:57.081 --> 00:04:59.600
proving technique and
constraints solving technique.

00:04:59.601 --> 00:05:03.905
So this is the overview
of the system.

00:05:03.906 --> 00:05:08.685
So, essentially for two SQL queries,

00:05:08.686 --> 00:05:12.670
we have two sort of
our execution pipeline in parallel.

00:05:12.671 --> 00:05:16.030
The first is we compel
these two SQL queries into

00:05:16.031 --> 00:05:19.510
propositions in
Interactive Theorem Prover.

00:05:19.511 --> 00:05:22.895
Then, we developed a proof search

00:05:22.896 --> 00:05:25.880
or we call it semidiscrete procedure

00:05:25.881 --> 00:05:32.300
to generate proofs for this
specific set of SQL equivalences.

00:05:32.301 --> 00:05:36.480
Well, if the Interactive
Theorem Prover is a proof,

00:05:36.481 --> 00:05:39.620
they are definitely equal like
for any possible given input.

00:05:39.621 --> 00:05:44.210
The second execution
pipeline in parallel

00:05:44.211 --> 00:05:49.920
is we also compel SQL query
into software constraint.

00:05:49.921 --> 00:05:54.320
So basically, we build
a model checker using rule set,

00:05:54.321 --> 00:05:56.630
so for 80 programming language that

00:05:56.631 --> 00:06:01.480
can find counter examples
for SQL queries.

00:06:01.481 --> 00:06:04.005
So basically, a
counter example means that

00:06:04.006 --> 00:06:07.505
a specific input such as

00:06:07.506 --> 00:06:10.640
these two queries will evaluate

00:06:10.641 --> 00:06:14.645
two different result and this is
clearly shows the equivalences.

00:06:14.646 --> 00:06:22.995
So this is a high level picture
of this Automated SQL Solver.

00:06:22.996 --> 00:06:25.470
So let's take a step back.

00:06:25.471 --> 00:06:28.430
So the first problem
before even we are

00:06:28.431 --> 00:06:31.790
talking about the
Automated SQL Reasoning,

00:06:31.791 --> 00:06:33.500
the first thing we need to address is

00:06:33.501 --> 00:06:36.810
actually the semantic for SQL.

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:41.820
At the beginning of this project,

00:06:41.821 --> 00:06:44.280
we ask ourselves, what is SQL?

00:06:44.281 --> 00:06:48.420
So if you ask people what is SQL,

00:06:48.421 --> 00:06:55.020
is actually this NC
standardization document. So.

00:06:55.021 --> 00:06:56.490
&gt;&gt; NC standardization document.

00:06:56.491 --> 00:06:59.000
&gt;&gt; Yes. You can notice
that there is more

00:06:59.001 --> 00:07:02.270
than like 1,700 pages
and really in English.

00:07:02.271 --> 00:07:06.980
Well, I think most people will
agree that this is insufficient for

00:07:06.981 --> 00:07:11.615
a rigorous SQL semantics and there
are many subtle issues there.

00:07:11.616 --> 00:07:16.940
Okay. Maybe in the future,

00:07:16.941 --> 00:07:18.440
we can use AI to generate

00:07:18.441 --> 00:07:20.540
like a SQL language model
using the same,

00:07:20.541 --> 00:07:22.970
but this is currently not
another current status.

00:07:22.971 --> 00:07:29.225
Then, we look back to
the origin of SQL.

00:07:29.226 --> 00:07:33.205
So if you take a very high level
point of view is SQL,

00:07:33.206 --> 00:07:35.030
is actually not that complicated.

00:07:35.031 --> 00:07:38.360
Well first, the data model
of SQL is relations.

00:07:38.361 --> 00:07:40.835
So usually, in practical
database systems,

00:07:40.836 --> 00:07:46.890
a relation is a collection of
tuples or a multi-site of tuples.

00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:51.045
Then, a relation has a schema,

00:07:51.046 --> 00:07:53.255
which is a set of attributes that

00:07:53.256 --> 00:07:57.955
each tuple in this
relation must obey to.

00:07:57.956 --> 00:08:00.495
Like super high level point of view,

00:08:00.496 --> 00:08:04.415
a SQL query just a
transformation function

00:08:04.416 --> 00:08:09.065
from a set of
interrelations to output,

00:08:09.066 --> 00:08:11.120
which is also relation.

00:08:11.121 --> 00:08:16.250
So that common underlying

00:08:16.251 --> 00:08:18.470
abstract semantic for SQL

00:08:18.471 --> 00:08:22.055
using database communities
of relational algebra.

00:08:22.056 --> 00:08:27.795
So, I will take a brief introduction
to relational algebra.

00:08:27.796 --> 00:08:30.555
So basically relational algebra,

00:08:30.556 --> 00:08:34.040
the original variable
relational algebra is

00:08:34.041 --> 00:08:39.445
defined over these five
operators over relations.

00:08:39.446 --> 00:08:42.650
So, people used to

00:08:42.651 --> 00:08:45.650
debating whether it should be
set semantic and back semantic.

00:08:45.651 --> 00:08:48.830
Let's say, in practice,

00:08:48.831 --> 00:08:51.650
let's just use a back
semantic, for example.

00:08:51.651 --> 00:08:56.365
So, the first operator
is a Cartesian product.

00:08:56.366 --> 00:09:00.290
So basically, it generate

00:09:00.291 --> 00:09:06.865
all possible pairs of
the tuples from two relations.

00:09:06.866 --> 00:09:09.900
The second, for example,

00:09:09.901 --> 00:09:12.400
select star from course and student,

00:09:12.401 --> 00:09:15.630
and the second operator
is a selection,

00:09:15.631 --> 00:09:21.030
is basically apply a future
predicate over relation.

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:24.390
Third called projection.

00:09:24.391 --> 00:09:30.690
It's transform a relation
from one form to another.

00:09:30.691 --> 00:09:33.330
While in the output relation,

00:09:33.331 --> 00:09:37.325
it has less in project
out some columns.

00:09:37.326 --> 00:09:40.700
For example, if there
is a relation name has

00:09:40.701 --> 00:09:44.150
a schema first name and last name,

00:09:44.151 --> 00:09:48.710
you can do a projection only project
the first name, for example.

00:09:48.711 --> 00:09:52.190
There's a force operator is union.

00:09:52.191 --> 00:09:55.790
So here, we're talking about
back semantic unit here,

00:09:55.791 --> 00:10:01.455
is basically, you just like
concatenated two relations together.

00:10:01.456 --> 00:10:05.625
The last one is a like a difference.

00:10:05.626 --> 00:10:13.575
So basically, I think in
mathematics, the cada monos.

00:10:13.576 --> 00:10:19.715
It's like a stat is a different
operator over two backs.

00:10:19.716 --> 00:10:23.030
People usually call these two,

00:10:23.031 --> 00:10:30.710
the SQL only containing
the first four operators,

00:10:30.711 --> 00:10:32.405
unions of conjunctive queries,

00:10:32.406 --> 00:10:36.845
and this is actually like
a proving decidable,

00:10:36.846 --> 00:10:40.655
but that seems to be
the end of story.

00:10:40.656 --> 00:10:44.260
But why I'm still here?
So unfortunately,

00:10:44.261 --> 00:10:49.860
there are like more operator
related in practice. So first.

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:53.485
Although by default,

00:10:53.486 --> 00:10:58.030
it's like a SQL database assumes
that relation is a back,

00:10:58.031 --> 00:11:03.310
but there is a explicit duplicate
elimination operator distinct,

00:11:03.311 --> 00:11:04.630
or in relation to algebra,

00:11:04.631 --> 00:11:08.785
they're called Delta, that sort
of convert them back to set.

00:11:08.786 --> 00:11:11.680
So, in practice, the SQL is

00:11:11.681 --> 00:11:15.490
running like a kind of mixed
bag and said semantics.

00:11:15.491 --> 00:11:17.680
Also as a result,

00:11:17.681 --> 00:11:21.100
a "Group By" which is grouping

00:11:21.101 --> 00:11:22.750
a relation and you can

00:11:22.751 --> 00:11:26.840
apply like aggregation
function over the grouping.

00:11:31.010 --> 00:11:34.480
In addition, in practice,

00:11:34.481 --> 00:11:36.790
people usually has
this Phi constraint.

00:11:36.791 --> 00:11:41.515
For example, keys, foreign
keys, et cetera, et cetera.

00:11:41.516 --> 00:11:45.175
So, I think the real
problem here is that

00:11:45.176 --> 00:11:51.100
like there is no [inaudible] like
meta-theory for relational algebra, right?

00:11:51.101 --> 00:11:52.570
So, if we're reasoning

00:11:52.571 --> 00:11:55.795
about the semantic equivalence
of two SQL queries,

00:11:55.796 --> 00:11:58.310
we sort needs such a meta-theory.

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:02.620
I mean, relational
algebra is nice but also

00:12:02.621 --> 00:12:05.050
this is a leaking abstraction
in the sense that,

00:12:05.051 --> 00:12:09.890
what is the semantic
of relational algebra?

00:12:10.590 --> 00:12:18.370
So, there is an existing
work on this landscape.

00:12:18.371 --> 00:12:20.770
For example, who actually have

00:12:20.771 --> 00:12:25.345
a Coq Formalization of
relational algebra.

00:12:25.346 --> 00:12:28.000
The basic idea is,

00:12:28.001 --> 00:12:31.330
why not formalize relation as a list?

00:12:31.331 --> 00:12:34.960
Right. That's normally how people

00:12:34.961 --> 00:12:40.310
formalize like a programming
language semantic in Coq.

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:44.830
Then, two queries are
equivalent if they are

00:12:44.831 --> 00:12:46.165
returning the same result for

00:12:46.166 --> 00:12:48.625
all possible input relations
is also a list.

00:12:48.626 --> 00:12:51.775
So basically, this is
a inductive argument.

00:12:51.776 --> 00:12:54.820
Well, but the problem is
that you need to reason

00:12:54.821 --> 00:12:58.375
about two lists are
equivalent, two permutations.

00:12:58.376 --> 00:13:01.000
So, you can't see that the equivalent

00:13:01.001 --> 00:13:03.520
to predicate here is
already pretty complicated,

00:13:03.521 --> 00:13:05.860
is basically a fixed
point operation over

00:13:05.861 --> 00:13:09.970
list over inductive structure.

00:13:09.971 --> 00:13:12.565
So, I'll give a brief example here.

00:13:12.566 --> 00:13:16.825
Right? So, think about
these two simple SQL queries.

00:13:16.826 --> 00:13:22.540
The first one you union
two relations R and S,

00:13:22.541 --> 00:13:25.345
and then apply a future predicate.

00:13:25.346 --> 00:13:28.210
On the second one, you apply

00:13:28.211 --> 00:13:33.580
a future predicate B over R and
S first and then union them.

00:13:33.581 --> 00:13:37.630
These two queries clarity
should be the same.

00:13:37.631 --> 00:13:42.955
So, think about if you are using
a model relational to the list.

00:13:42.956 --> 00:13:47.380
So basically, you need to
do a lot of inductions.

00:13:47.381 --> 00:13:49.045
So, like normally people do is

00:13:49.046 --> 00:13:53.440
first do a induction on R and then do

00:13:53.441 --> 00:13:55.570
an induction on S. This is kind of

00:13:55.571 --> 00:14:00.730
a less inductions and for
the equivalent to predicates,

00:14:00.731 --> 00:14:04.120
there's another induction
on the output relation.

00:14:04.121 --> 00:14:07.600
So, this is known to be
a super hard problem

00:14:07.601 --> 00:14:11.530
even in the programming language
and formal math research.

00:14:11.531 --> 00:14:21.040
So, using this a relation
at least semantics,

00:14:21.041 --> 00:14:24.220
you can prove some
simple SQL rewrite.

00:14:24.221 --> 00:14:30.295
So, actually for query like
item potent of selection,

00:14:30.296 --> 00:14:33.880
like the author used like 40 lines of

00:14:33.881 --> 00:14:37.885
Coq code to prove
this very simple SQL rewrite.

00:14:37.886 --> 00:14:42.055
Also, due to the
complexity of the proof,

00:14:42.056 --> 00:14:45.145
very limited rewrite has been
proven under this semantic.

00:14:45.146 --> 00:14:48.610
So, this is clearly not going
anywhere like if you want

00:14:48.611 --> 00:14:52.780
to develop an automated
reasoning system for SQL.

00:14:52.781 --> 00:14:55.705
So here, we come up
with different idea.

00:14:55.706 --> 00:14:59.305
Well, the core of our idea is,

00:14:59.306 --> 00:15:03.385
so why not model relation
just a function?

00:15:03.386 --> 00:15:08.200
Because here, we only

00:15:08.201 --> 00:15:11.510
care about the equivalence
of two SQL queries.

00:15:12.710 --> 00:15:15.360
Unlike the previous work is that we

00:15:15.361 --> 00:15:18.490
actually need to extract a system.

00:15:21.950 --> 00:15:25.560
The idea here is that we model

00:15:25.561 --> 00:15:29.080
relation as a function
from a tuple to,

00:15:29.081 --> 00:15:31.120
let's say, a larger number.

00:15:31.121 --> 00:15:34.390
I will claim this is really is like

00:15:34.391 --> 00:15:40.340
how a relation or multiset should
be modeled mathematically.

00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:48.460
Then, we model predicates as

00:15:48.461 --> 00:15:53.515
a function from a tuple to
either zero or one, right?

00:15:53.516 --> 00:15:59.305
And then this SQL query could
be modeled by a Select,

00:15:59.306 --> 00:16:02.470
apply the future predicate
to the relation could be

00:16:02.471 --> 00:16:05.710
modeled by the multiplication of

00:16:05.711 --> 00:16:07.000
the evaluation result of

00:16:07.001 --> 00:16:13.659
the predicate and times
the original multiplicity,

00:16:13.660 --> 00:16:18.860
all numbers from appearance of
this tuple in this relation.

00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:27.880
Then, union like
back semantic union is

00:16:27.881 --> 00:16:33.350
simply the addition of
these multiplicities.

00:16:35.850 --> 00:16:39.190
&gt;&gt; Is joining natural join
looks like you're duplicating

00:16:39.191 --> 00:16:42.580
tuples in this third
formula right here,

00:16:42.581 --> 00:16:46.690
mapping [inaudible] them correctly.

00:16:46.691 --> 00:16:49.330
&gt;&gt; It's a Cartesian product.

00:16:49.331 --> 00:16:51.610
There is no joint here,
right? The third one.

00:16:51.611 --> 00:16:55.900
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, if it's
a Cartesian product,

00:16:55.901 --> 00:17:00.130
then don't you end up with
two copies of the tuple in

00:17:00.131 --> 00:17:06.070
the Select star from R?

00:17:06.071 --> 00:17:08.065
I don't clearly understand
the function but-

00:17:08.066 --> 00:17:10.300
&gt;&gt; So basically, the

00:17:10.301 --> 00:17:13.945
predicate evaluate to
either zero or one, right?

00:17:13.946 --> 00:17:18.625
So let's say this- try to
model the output relation.

00:17:18.626 --> 00:17:21.985
&gt;&gt; What does it mean to
a Cartesian product of a function?

