This automated grading tool
is deploying you like

00:58:20.131 --> 00:58:26.760
a two database management
classes since last year

00:58:26.761 --> 00:58:31.545
and this work has won
a signal best demo and

00:58:31.546 --> 00:58:35.025
happens to be the top one
training record project

00:58:35.026 --> 00:58:37.305
in GitHub and my joke is that,

00:58:37.306 --> 00:58:40.755
if you want to make
a top one training GitHub project,

00:58:40.756 --> 00:58:43.440
you need to use
a less popular language.

00:58:43.441 --> 00:58:50.235
So, in conclusion,

00:58:50.236 --> 00:58:55.065
I present Cosette as
the first practical SQL solver.

00:58:55.066 --> 00:59:00.525
Cosette is based on
a new axiomatic semantics for SQL.

00:59:00.526 --> 00:59:03.570
We implemented
semi-decision procedure

00:59:03.571 --> 00:59:07.350
for unions of conjunctive queries
and they're both set in

00:59:07.351 --> 00:59:11.160
bag semantics with
integrated constraint and

00:59:11.161 --> 00:59:16.035
the core idea of
this architecture of Cosette is,

00:59:16.036 --> 00:59:18.960
we actually integrate
both interactive theorem

00:59:18.961 --> 00:59:20.985
proving and constraints solving,

00:59:20.986 --> 00:59:23.535
to solve these really hard problem.

00:59:23.536 --> 00:59:27.570
In addition, I believe
that Automated reasoning

00:59:27.571 --> 00:59:30.870
brought by formal methods
plus dummy specific semantic.

00:59:30.871 --> 00:59:33.240
The world needs more reliable

00:59:33.241 --> 00:59:47.400
and more optimize future
of the data systems. Yes.

00:59:47.401 --> 00:59:50.520
&gt;&gt; So, for the assignment grading,

00:59:50.521 --> 00:59:54.525
what is this task that
the students are actually solving,

00:59:54.526 --> 00:59:55.830
that this is checking them?

00:59:55.831 --> 00:59:56.400
&gt;&gt; Sorry?

00:59:56.401 --> 00:59:58.845
&gt;&gt; What is the task that
the students are solving?

00:59:58.846 --> 01:00:00.525
Are they given one of the queries

01:00:00.526 --> 01:00:03.705
and they try to run
it at other queries?

01:00:03.706 --> 01:00:06.720
&gt;&gt; Given the English description,

01:00:06.721 --> 01:00:11.160
write a database query.
So, then we test

01:00:11.161 --> 01:00:13.605
the student answer against
the standard answer, right?

01:00:13.606 --> 01:00:15.070
&gt;&gt; Okay.

01:00:15.560 --> 01:00:23.400
&gt;&gt; I mean, we find that the
automated grader actually can find

01:00:23.401 --> 01:00:28.380
tricky cases that the TA for example,

01:00:28.381 --> 01:00:31.200
cannot find or TA or
graduate student cannot find,

01:00:31.201 --> 01:00:39.300
but there is downside. At
end of day, for the grading,

01:00:39.301 --> 01:00:43.110
you need to give partial credit
and we still working on how to

01:00:43.111 --> 01:00:45.450
give partial credit for student

01:00:45.451 --> 01:00:47.310
for the unequal and
equal SQL queries.

01:00:47.311 --> 01:00:51.070
That would be a pretty interesting
research topic as well.

01:00:53.360 --> 01:00:57.210
&gt;&gt; Have you consider
the case that sometimes,

01:00:57.211 --> 01:00:59.650
the equivalence is dead?

01:01:01.040 --> 01:01:05.565
&gt;&gt; Well, then they are not
universally equivalent, right.

01:01:05.566 --> 01:01:07.935
&gt;&gt; So, why can't it be?

01:01:07.936 --> 01:01:09.810
&gt;&gt; Can you give an example?

01:01:09.811 --> 01:01:12.420
&gt;&gt; Example is how
the truncates depends on

01:01:12.421 --> 01:01:17.440
the actual data to decide if
they are equivalents or not.

01:01:17.750 --> 01:01:26.415
&gt;&gt; So, I will say, it's
a different equivalent criteria.

01:01:26.416 --> 01:01:31.530
I think one way to
address this problem is

01:01:31.531 --> 01:01:37.230
that you have a way to let the user

01:01:37.231 --> 01:01:41.895
express these sort of data semantic

01:01:41.896 --> 01:01:46.920
formally and then I think
it's possible that we

01:01:46.921 --> 01:01:49.365
extend to that work
to handle such cases.

01:01:49.366 --> 01:01:54.990
In fact, we do know
a more general data semantic.

01:01:54.991 --> 01:01:56.865
For example, there is work from

01:01:56.866 --> 01:02:01.209
Edenburg behave like
a conditional functional dependency.

01:02:01.670 --> 01:02:06.975
I think it's interesting
to see, can we express it?

01:02:06.976 --> 01:02:10.740
But, on the other hand
practically speaking,

01:02:10.760 --> 01:02:13.935
in the real world SQL databases,

01:02:13.936 --> 01:02:17.100
there is no way for the user

01:02:17.101 --> 01:02:21.340
to specify such kind
of data semantics.

